      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the number of different orders in which Jordan and his three best friends can run the relay team, given that Jordan is running the fourth lap, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the runners**: The team has four members: Jordan, his first best friend, his second best friend, and his third best friend.\n2. **Fix Jordan's position**: Since Jordan must run the fourth lap, we only need to determine the order of the other three runners. The fourth lap is fixed for Jordan, so we need to arrange the first, second, and third runners out of the remaining four members.\n3. **Choose the first runner**: There are 3 choices for who can run the first lap (any one of the three best friends except Jordan).\n4. **Choose the second runner**: After the first runner has been chosen, there are 2 remaining choices for who can run the second lap.\n5. **Choose the third runner**: After the first two runners have been chosen, there is only 1 remaining choice for who can run the third lap.\n\nThe number of different orders can be calculated by multiplying the number of choices for each position:\n\\[ 3 \\text{ (choices for the first runner)} \\times 2 \\text{ (choices for the second runner)} \\times 1 \\text{ (choice for the third runner)} = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6. \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different orders in which the four team members can run, given that Jordan runs the fourth lap, is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
