      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of different orders in which Jordan's relay team can run the race, with the constraint that Jordan runs the fourth lap, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Fix Jordan's position:**\n   Since Jordan is running the fourth lap, we only need to determine the positions for the other three team members. This reduces the problem to finding the number of ways to arrange 3 people in 3 positions.\n\n2. **Calculate the number of arrangements:**\n   The number of ways to arrange 3 people in 3 positions is given by the factorial of 3, denoted as \\(3!\\). The factorial of a number \\(n\\) is the product of all positive integers up to \\(n\\).\n\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n\n3. **List all possible arrangements:**\n   To verify, let's list all possible permutations of the three team members A, B, and C. The permutations are:\n   - ABC\n   - ACB\n   - BAC\n   - BCA\n   - CAB\n   - CBA\n\n   There are indeed 6 different arrangements.\n\nTherefore, the number of different orders in which the four team members can run, assuming that Jordan runs the fourth lap, is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
